#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078154 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b08650da-89e7-5a9d-89a6-de3623344662" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "aea27069-47b4-50b0-9e70-3a65951916b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-291" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(-2) = 0$ and $f'(-2)=2$. Estimate the value of $f(-1.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x - (-2)) \\\\\n&= 0 + 2(x + 2) \\\\\n&= 2(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-1.8$. We get\n\n$$\nf(-1.8) \\mid \\approx L(-1.8); = 2(-1.8 + 2); = 2(0.2); = 0.4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078155 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "44510fee-857c-5182-8f9e-a08c18966142" :question/math-academy-id "e-292" :question/problem "Let $g$ be a function such that $g'(x) =\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(4) = 3$. Using the tangent line approximation of $g(x)$ at $x=4$, estimate the value of $g(3.6)$." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $g(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= g(a) + g^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= g(4) + g^{′}(4)(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $g(4)=3$, and since\n\n$$\ng'(x)=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\ng'(4)=\\sqrt{4}=2\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= 3 + 2(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $g(3.6)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.6$. We get\n\n$$\ng(3.6) \\mid \\approx L(3.6); = 3 + 2(3.6 - 4); = 3 + 2(-0.4); = 3 - 0.8; = 2.2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078159 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/id #uuid "dc311e80-fd79-5b89-9f0e-0b1caef6457e" :question/math-academy-id "e-293" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-2,-5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0)$. If $f'(-2)=3$, estimate the value of $f(-2.3)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x - (-2)) \\\\\n&= f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(-2,-5)$, so we have\n\n$$\nf(-2)=-5\n$$\n\nAlso, we're told that $f'(-2)=3$, so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= -5 + 3(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-2.3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-2.3$. We get\n\n$$\nf(-2.3) \\mid \\approx L(-2.3); =-5 + 3(-2.3 + 2); =-5 + 3(-0.3); =-5 - 0.9; =-5.9\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-\\infty,0)$, the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=-2$, and therefore we have an overestimate."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5e12493-b3d1-503e-aeeb-62a61da2a054" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(2.2) \\approx 2.8$, an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076522} {:answer/id #uuid "2549d6ce-7cf5-5fee-a27d-d3c401b31b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(2.2) \\approx 2.9$, an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076523} {:answer/id #uuid "303d8c0e-c99f-5ec3-a237-6a2b9dfaba77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(2.2) \\approx 2.8$, an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076524} {:answer/id #uuid "41a29dd4-a33f-5537-b8b4-73069048410a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(2.2) \\approx 3.2$, an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076525} {:answer/id #uuid "54e3ffcf-7341-59ad-be04-5d2142becf01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(2.2) \\approx 3.2$, an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "303d8c0e-c99f-5ec3-a237-6a2b9dfaba77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(2.2) \\approx 2.8$, an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076524} :answer-field/id #uuid "0871af03-0f0d-58d4-a5c7-fa0f71cc2bf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076521}] :question/id #uuid "411c84d4-98f5-597f-b905-67ed62bcac2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-47782" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe graph of the function $y = f(x)$ passes through $(2, 3)$ and is concave up on the interval $(0, 3)$. If $f^{′}(2) =-1$, estimate the value of $f(2.2)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x = 2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076d7f5d-b617-5e78-aaf7-f2061f4571ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076570} {:answer/id #uuid "16fc4fd2-9ceb-58df-9cc2-2902abe90602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076571} {:answer/id #uuid "35e091b9-b4ee-5b3e-bd55-ffdf655dd494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076572} {:answer/id #uuid "5dea0cfc-d406-5bf9-bd96-62ebe1d09a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076573} {:answer/id #uuid "75aa9f1d-b2e0-5cd5-80cb-177a2d9d4a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35e091b9-b4ee-5b3e-bd55-ffdf655dd494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076572} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fccc738-3448-5c35-8edc-6b73ee47ad96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076569}] :question/id #uuid "c639e205-a1df-57d0-bade-b6750f88ba37" :question/math-academy-id "q-4804" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nLet $f$ be a function such that $f^{′}(x) = x^{2} - 4$ and $f(4) = 13$. Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x = 4$ to estimate the value of $f(3.5)$." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
